// @check
// @error-at 17:10 expected `,` but found `2`
// @error-at 18:15 cannot assign string to 'n' of type i32
// STAGE 2, FIRST CONVERSION: a missing list separator is INSERTED, never skipped
// past, so the recovery is faithful and the file is typechecked. `f(1 2)` is the
// two-argument call the user wrote — the second diagnostic below proves the
// checker ran over it, and the ABSENT one proves the tree is right: no
// `wrong number of arguments: expected 2, got 1`, which is what the old
// skip-to-the-closer recovery would have made the checker say once the bail was
// lifted. That phantom was pinned in `tests/vl_lossless_recovery_test.ts` until
// this landed.
//
// `parser/call-missing-comma-recovers.vl` is the SAME call beside an `o.x++`,
// whose recovery is still lossy — one lossy diagnostic anywhere in a file
// restores the bail for the whole file, so that one reports no type error at
// all. The two cases together are the ALL quantifier at work.
function f(a: i32, b: i32) { a + b }
print(f(1 2))
const n: i32 = "hi"
print(n)
